@node "Amiga" "Amiga Corporation"
@{JCENTER}@{IMG "GUIDEDIR:files/32seonliner.png"}

@{IMG "GUIDEDIR:files/newAmiga_logo-sm.jpeg"}

Out-of-court settlement between Amiga and Hyperion
Amiga Corporation announces completion of its own RTG system
Also the Amiga logo has been updated, looks really good!


@{IMG "GUIDEDIR:files/thea1200sm.jpg"}

Retro Games Ltd., Amiga's most important licensee at present, 
the THEA1200, would also support RTG.
Notice the changes on the box where Amiga logo is now included!


For the first time, Hyperion is also being granted an explicit 
license to distribute AmigaOS 3.2 as well as the yet-to-be-released 
AmigaOS 3.3 - however, this license expires on December 31, 2027, 
after which Hyperion must withdraw from the 68k market.

Sometime 2028
I'm sure Mike (Amiga) already working on a plan for the Amiga,
there is some plans for the next AmigaOS maybe v3.28 ?? (Year 28),
this is to make it clear on the latest AmigaOS version, I think
this idea is great, I hope this is the way Mike goes for.


Now I have included new about Commodore 77, not Amiga related but
Mike has been/is in talks with Christian (Commodore) and I think
we are going to see something Amiga related in future from
Commodore. It would be awesome with a Commodore Amiga A77 ;)


@{IMG "GUIDEDIR:files/a77-sm.png"}
A.I mockup !

Now I have to speculate a bit, I think a pure FPGA doesn't cut it,
just like C77 goes for crazy 77MHz and this is 77x original C64.
On Amiga you would not get this increase with a FPGA, we want 77x
68060 50MHz (best CPU at time) so I'm thinking like do AGA with
68EC020 where CPU part can be switched over to ARM that does CPU
emulation, beside this optional CPU.. ARM should handle WIFI, BT
+ other usefull things that are not available or too expensive/
time consuming to develop in a FPGA or other specific chips.

Time will tell what we get!


@{FG shine}
*** ***
*********
**** ****
*******
*****
*
@{FG text}


@{JLEFT}
@endnode 




@node "C77" "Commodore 77"
@{JCENTER}@{IMG "GUIDEDIR:files/32seonliner.png"}

@{IMG "GUIDEDIR:files/Commodore-77-sm.jpg"}
Play music @{"CyberPunk 2077 24-channel SID" SYSTEM "play16 Q files/c77.wav" STRICT}

@{JLEFT}
  Commodore 77
  ____________

  The rain falls. Neon burns through the darkness.
  Somewhere beyond the reach of the corps, a
  Commodore 77 boots to life. Inspired by the world of 
  Cyberpunk 2077® and built on the legendary 
  Commodore 64 Ultimate retro computer - for 10,000+ 
  retro games, BASIC coding, and distraction-free writing
  
  - it features an exclusive Seocho Electronics edition 
  exterior design inspired by one of the most recognizable 
  manufacturers in Night City.

  Made for those who refuse to hand every file 
  and function to the corps, it delivers authentic FPGA-
  powered computing without corporate control, endless 
  feeds, or algorithmic distractions. Just the freedom to 
  write, code, play, and create on your own terms - all at 
  up to 77MHz processing speed.

  In a city where connection is the trap, the Commodore 
  77 proves that sometimes the safest place for your data 
  is right where you left it. 

  The Commodore 77 collectible special edition includes:

  · 'Shard' cartridge holding an exclusive 8-Bit 'demo'
    Cyberpunk experience, from Commodore x Arise 
    Designs, with 24-channel stereo SID music by LukHash
  
  · Upgraded AMD Artix 7 XC7A100T processor for 
    77MHz top speed

  · Themed spiral bound user guide with 
    Cyberpunk 2077 inspired type-in BASIC programs
    + lore

  · Exclusive Commodore x Cyberpunk 2077 enamel 
    pins + stickers

  · Extra-long HDMI cable to jack in with

  · World power adapter (compatible with Night City)



  
@{JCENTER}
Ohhh my god this is so nice I wish Amiga got this attention
to details. Should I add one anyway to my collection ? ;)


